  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMid and upper level low over the Eastern Great Lakes to track east\ninto New England overnight. Low clouds over the area will erode from\nwest to east late in the day thru this evening. In the wake of the\nlow a northwesterly flow aloft will develop with surface high\npressure over the Mid MS Valley building into the TN Valley\novernight. Clearing skies and light winds will result in chilly\novernight low temperatures. Expect lows to range from the upper 20s\nto the lower 30s. Have continued the freeze warning for tonight and \nSunday morning for counties where the frost/freeze season continues. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nNorthwest flow aloft will continue Sunday and then back more westerly\nearly next week. As surface high pressure builds to our southeast a \na southwest low level flow will develop. As a result, a dry forecast\nis expected through mid week with highs moderating from the 50s on\nSunday and Monday into the lower and middle 60s Tuesday. \n\nA digging system will result in the passage of a surface cold front \nlate Wednesday into Thursday. This system leads to a mid level low \nthat develops over the Great Lakes late in the week. This will lead \nto a chance of rain beginning Wednesday night and continuing into \nThursday. The mid level low will keep the threat for precipitation in\nthe forecast into Friday. Temperatures will turn colder with highs \nin the 50s Thursday, the 40s for Friday, and the upper 40s to around \n50 Saturday.",
